          "text": "A common question is: 'Can we trust that the Bible we read today accurately represents what was originally written?' The answer from textual criticism is a resounding yes, with important nuances.\n\nFirst, the sheer number of manuscripts is unparalleled. We have approximately 5,800 Greek New Testament manuscripts, plus thousands more in Latin, Syriac, Coptic, and other ancient languages. For comparison, Homer's Iliad-the most well-attested ancient secular work-has fewer than 1,900 manuscripts, and most classical works survive in only a handful of copies.\n\nSecond, the early date of manuscripts gives us confidence. For the New Testament, we have fragments from the 2nd century and complete manuscripts from the 4th century-remarkably close to the original composition (1st century AD). Most classical works have gaps of 500-1,000 years between composition and earliest surviving manuscript.\n\nThird, the variants themselves demonstrate reliability. While there are approximately 400,000 textual variants among all manuscripts, this number is inflated because it counts every instance in every manuscript. If one spelling difference appears in 3,000 manuscripts, it's counted 3,000 times. The vast majority of variants are insignificant: spelling, word order, or synonyms that don't affect meaning. Meaningful variants are few, and viable variants that could change interpretation are even fewer.\n\nMost importantly, no essential Christian doctrine depends on any disputed variant. The deity of Christ, His virgin birth, His substitutionary atonement, His bodily resurrection, salvation by grace through faith-all these doctrines are established by multiple, well-attested passages. Even if every disputed variant was resolved against traditional readings, Christian theology would remain unchanged.\n\nTextual criticism, therefore, is not an enemy of faith but a tool that demonstrates the remarkable preservation of God's Word through history."
